A large language model (LLM) works by breaking text into tokens, mapping them into embeddings, and using the Transformer architecture with self‑attention to predict the next token in a sequence. It is trained first on massive text datasets for general language patterns, then fine‑tuned for specific tasks like chat or coding. LLMs excel at generating fluent, context‑aware text but can also hallucinate or reflect biases from their training data.

